{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794390552,"nonce":"mtGuAVNnyTZm7j+mfqoZ7LDJctw=","message":"Fiber cold-compile follow-up (69f9d628). The 2x cold-launch regression is specific to the unit suite, not to Pocket Studio.\n\nProfile of wasmtime compiling the unit suite's no-LLVM fiber module: register allocation dominates (regalloc2's main loop is the top non-idle frame by a wide margin). The fiber transform's single unwind exit reads every local, so every local is live across every call site of an instrumented function; cost grows with locals x call sites. The unit suite has giant test functions (up to 88 locals x 3469 calls in one body), which LLVM's output avoids because wasm LLVM keeps single-use temporaries on the operand stack (3.6 locals per function against 6.3 here).\n\nPocket Studio, cold, `ash-wasm-run` compiling before it stops at the missing HDLLs (load ~9): fibers LLVM 7.6s wall / 18.1s CPU, no-LLVM 7.1s / 16.2s; no fibers LLVM 1.7s / 6.5s, no-LLVM 1.8s / 5.4s. So for the target program the build is ~3x faster (14.1s -\u003e 4.8s wall at load 31-55) and the cold compile is the same. hello is the same too.\n\nIf the unit-suite shape matters: keep single-use temporaries on the operand stack in the emitter (register stackification), restricted to spans with no call, to cut locals per function; or save only live locals in the transform. Neither is done. V8 (the browser host) compiles with Liftoff and is not measured.","files":null}]}